];

const faq = [
  { q: 'Do ravens play pranks on other animals?', a: 'Ravens engage in behaviours that look like pranks: they sneak up on resting dogs or wolves and tug their tails, then hop back to avoid a reaction; they steal objects within sight of humans or other ravens and carry them conspicuously, apparently soliciting a chase; they emit alarm calls without apparent danger, sending food-competitors fleeing so the raven can steal their food. Whether these constitute "pranks" in the sense requiring intent to amuse depends on interpretation, but the behaviours are well-documented and appear playfully calculated rather than purely food-driven.' },
  { q: 'Do ravens laugh?', a: 'Ravens do not produce a sound conventionally identified as laughter, but they have multiple vocalizations associated with play: a soft, rapid "knocking" call produced during social play; a range of non-distress, low-intensity calls exchanged between affiliative individuals. They also produce sounds researchers describe as "gurgling" or "knocking" in relaxed, playful contexts. Whether any of these constitutes a functional analogue to laughter (a vocal signal of positive affect during play) is an open research question.' },
  { q: 'Are ravens mischievous birds?', a: 'Ravens are frequently described as mischievous by researchers and long-term observers, and the description fits: they investigate, manipulate, steal, and play with objects and other animals in ways that exceed what food-seeking alone would predict. Bernd Heinrich\'s accounts of young ravens at his Vermont forest camp describe birds that persistently investigated objects, hid and retrieved them, manipulated tarps and strings, and solicited play with the researcher. This exploratory, apparently playful excess of behaviour relative to immediate needs is a consistent feature of raven reports across cultures and contexts.' },
];
